From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 03:07:05 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 118/148] hjviewer: phones and tablets choose their layout by
 size (UX-M3)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

U64: the Android and iOS app keep the portrait (touch) layout in any
orientation and window (hjvPortraitLayout in main.dart's
_updateOrientation; the web keeps choosing by aspect), so a phone held
landscape, an iPad or a split screen never gets the desktop's 220x140
cards, hover border and 28 px menu. A phone (display shortest side
under 600) holds its home in portrait: HjvOrientationObserver counts
the pages on the app's navigator and asks portraitUp while the home is
the only page, every declared orientation under a page above it (the
remote screen, file transfer, terminal, camera, settings sub-screens);
dialogs and popup menus are not pages. Tablets are not held. Where the
system ignores the hold (Android split screen and free windows), a
window 500 high or lower gets a 44 px app bar. G3 is already one line
(U04); the sliver peer list of the item's first proposal is not done
(design T7 chose the portrait hold instead).

U65: on a tablet the connection page (ID field, G3, tab bar, list) is
one column in the middle, at most 810 wide like the settings; the ID
field follows the column instead of the 600 kMobilePageConstraints
(the web keeps 600). A tablet's peer list from 700 wide shows the touch
cards in two columns (hjvPeerColumns), in the same ListView.builder, a
row per item; a phone's list stays one card per row. My PCs and the
other tabs share this through peers_view.dart.

G20 (1.8.2 screen review carry-over): on a phone, S1's [지원 코드 만들기]
sits at the right of the My PCs panel's search row instead of a row of
its own above the panel, which made the tab about 48 px taller.

Tests: hjviewer_phone_test.dart, the size rules, the two columns, the
low app bar, and the observer with dialogs and pages on a phone and a
tablet.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
